WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.293
Python provides different randomness interfaces for different purposes.

2
00:00:04.293 --> 00:00:11.603
The general random module is useful for simulations, sampling, games,
shuffling, non-security choices, and repeatable tests.

3
00:00:11.603 --> 00:00:17.305
It is not intended for passwords, tokens, keys, reset links, session
identifiers, or other secrets.

4
00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:24.548
Using it in security-sensitive code creates risk because its design goals
are not the same as cryptographic unpredictability.

5
00:00:24.548 --> 00:00:29.914
The secrets module is a high-level interface intended for security-
sensitive random values.

6
00:00:29.914 --> 00:00:36.353
It helps developers generate tokens, choose from character sets, and work
with randomness suitable for secrets.

7
00:00:36.353 --> 00:00:38.835
System randomness concepts, including os.

8
00:00:38.835 --> 00:00:44.804
urandom and SystemRandom at a high level, rely on operating system
facilities designed for security use.

9
00:00:44.804 --> 00:00:52.652
The exact implementation details can change, but the selection principle
remains stable: choose the API based on the purpose of the value.

10
00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:56.810
Security-sensitive generation should be centralized where practical.

11
00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:02.444
If every route or utility function chooses its own randomness method,
mistakes become hard to find.

12
00:01:02.444 --> 00:01:09.352
A shared helper can express the approved pattern for reset tokens,
invitation links, API keys, and internal identifiers.

13
00:01:09.352 --> 00:01:17.066
Centralization also makes it easier to adjust length, encoding, storage,
expiration, and logging behavior when requirements change.

14
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:21.090
Code review should treat randomness API choice as a security decision.

15
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:28.535
Reviewers should ask whether the value grants access, proves identity,
protects cryptography, or controls a sensitive workflow.

16
00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:36.114
If yes, non-security random APIs, predictable seeds, counters, timestamps,
and custom string mixing should be treated as concerns.

17
00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:41.480
Safe selection is not about memorizing every function; it is about
matching the API to the risk.
